    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nI've watched more product roadmaps collapse than I can count, and it usually isn't because the ideas were bad. The real problem is that most teams treat the roadmap like a contract instead of a hypothesis. They lock in features and dates months ahead based on incomplete information, then act surprised when customers don't care, technology changes, or a competitor ships something better. Updating it feels like admitting failure, so the document slowly becomes fiction while the actual product evolves in a completely different direction.\n\nA lot of this comes from confusing outputs with outcomes. The typical roadmap is a list of things to build, not a set of problems to solve or results to achieve. Stakeholders demand their features appear on it, product managers add them to keep the peace, and suddenly the team is building a Frankenstein of requests instead of a coherent product. There's almost never enough time spent talking to users before those items get committed. Assumptions stay assumptions until it's too late and expensive to reverse.\n\nCapacity is another silent killer. Roadmaps rarely account for the messy reality of software: bugs, technical debt, onboarding, support, the unexpected production incident. Everything is estimated as if the team will operate at peak efficiency forever. When dates inevitably slip, leadership loses faith, engineering feels set up to fail, and the next planning cycle starts with even more cynicism.\n\nPolitics make it worse. The person with the most power or the loudest voice often dictates what goes on the page, regardless of data. Strategy gets diluted into a collection of unrelated bets. People also mix up a roadmap with a detailed project plan and expect precision that discovery work simply cannot provide.\n\nThe roadmaps that survive are usually shorter, more flexible, focused on themes or customer problems rather than specific features, and treated as conversation starters rather than sacred texts. Most organizations want the appearance of certainty more than they want actual learning. That's why the majority fail. They become artifacts of wishful thinking instead of tools for making better decisions.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n**Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail**\n\nProduct roadmaps are meant to align teams, set expectations, and guide execution toward valuable outcomes. Yet most fail—not because planning is inherently flawed, but because they are treated as rigid contracts rather than living hypotheses. The result is missed deadlines, wasted effort, eroded trust, and products that miss the market.\n\nA core reason is the absence of a clear strategy. Many roadmaps are feature lists disconnected from business goals or customer problems. Teams commit to shipping “the dashboard,” “the mobile app,” or “AI features” without defining success metrics or the underlying jobs-to-be-done. When priorities shift or data contradicts assumptions, the plan has no north star, so work continues on items that no longer matter.\n\nOvercommitment compounds the problem. Stakeholders demand dates and scope; product managers, seeking alignment, pack the timeline with more than capacity allows. Engineering realities—technical debt, unknowns, dependencies—are underestimated. The roadmap becomes a promise rather than a forecast, guaranteeing disappointment when delivery slips.\n\nPoor communication and lack of shared ownership accelerate failure. A roadmap created in isolation by product or leadership is rarely understood or believed by engineering, design, sales, or customers. Without regular, transparent updates and genuine input, it is ignored or gamed. Cross-functional dependencies surface too late, and political pressure to “hit the date” overrides learning.\n\nFinally, most roadmaps ignore change. Markets, competitors, user behavior, and technology evolve. Treating the document as static—rather than a set of bets that must be validated, killed, or adjusted—guarantees irrelevance. Teams that refuse to re-prioritize or cut scope when evidence arrives waste quarters on the original plan.\n\nSuccessful roadmaps invert these patterns: they start from outcomes, remain visibly tentative, involve the people who will execute them, and are revisited frequently with data. The ones that fail do the opposite. They confuse activity with progress and commitment with certainty. In a world of uncertainty, that is a recipe for waste.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
